for the last three months this type of  civil unrest has become the norm as  demonstrators protest ongoing  kidnappings and violence  as well as mostly stagnant wages in the  face of soaring inflation  protesters in port-au-prince say life is  becoming unattainable and they're  demanding that government officials do  more to address the problems facing them  on a daily basis  traders can't take to the streets our  children can't go to school because of  the kidnapping and crime rising costs  and inflation are only making things  worse for us  everything from food to fuel is getting  more expensive  bakeries that make the bread most  stations rely on every day could soon be  forced to shut down due to supply  shortages and rising costs  the war in ukraine has hurt us a lot  because imported flour plays such an  important role it has made it difficult  to get wheat into haiti that's why it's  so expensive for us even if we can't  afford it  haiti imports 70 of its food and the u.n  estimates that nearly half of the  population is in urgent need of  assistance  many of the medical workers who went on  strike earlier this month over security  concerns are refusing to return to work  until their wages are increased  we're aware that we're not supposed to  hold the strike for this long but it's  not our fault we've tried talking to the  government but they tell us they're not  able to increase our wages  haiti's most vulnerable are the ones  paying the highest cost as there are no  doctors or nurses to attend to their  needs  there were eight patients here with me  five already left the hospital with  infections because they couldn't find a  doctor we are asking the government to  solve the problems of the strikers  because we cannot suffer too much  anymore  the political paralysis in haiti has  only gotten worse since the july  assassination of former president  jovenel moyes  no date has yet been set for new  elections and the acting prime minister  says the first step is to hold a  constitutional referendum  tuesday protests marks the 35th  anniversary of haiti's current  constitution  and organizers say they will continue to  apply pressure until those in charge  move forward with a change the country  so desperately needs  teresa
 

